) = \\binom{n}{k} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, we are asked to find \\( P(10) \\), the probability of getting exactly 4 heads in 10 flips of a fair coin. Here, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 4 \\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the binomial coefficient \\( \\binom{10}{4} \\), which represents the number of ways to choose 4 heads out of 10 flips:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{4} = \\frac{10!}{4!(10-4)!} = \\frac{10!}{4! \\cdot 6!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7}{4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 210\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\( \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{10} \\), which is the probability of getting any specific sequence of 10 flips where exactly 4 of them are heads:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{10} = \\frac{1}{1024}\n\\]\n\nNow, we combine these results to find \\( P(10) \\):\n\n\\[\nP(10) = \\binom{10}{4} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{10} = 210 \\times \\frac{1}{1024} = \\frac{210}{1024} = \\frac{105}{512}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( P(10) \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{105}{512}}\n\\]\n\nA clue given during the AIME (American Invitational Mathematics Examination) is provided by the formula $p = \\frac{1}{3} + \\left\\lfloor \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2} n} \\right\\rfloor \\cdot \\{ \\frac{3}{2} \\ln n \\}$, where $p$ represents the percentage of a specific type of attraction in a study area excluding natural landforms. $\\{ \\cdot \\}$ signifies the fractional part of a number. Given that $p$ is a whole number, to evaluate the area's total attraction percentage, we need to find the least positive integer $n$ such that $\\frac{n}{200}$ is an integer. Remember, for $n$ to be integral, $n/200$ should be an integer. What is this smallest integer $n$?\n\nTo find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that \\( \\frac{n}{200} \\) is an integer, we need to determine the smallest \\( n \\) that is a multiple of 200. This means finding the least common multiple (LCM) of 200.\n\nThe prime factorization of 200 is:\n\\[ 200 = 2^3 \\times 5^2 \\]\n\nT